     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order because the dose looks unsafe.\n\nAcetaminophen 2000 mg three times daily comes to 6,000 mg/day. That is above the usual adult maximum of 4,000 mg/day, and many institutions cap it at 3,000 mg/day. The 2,000 mg single dose is also twice the usual maximum of 1,000 mg. Even with normal liver function, this raises the risk of hepatotoxicity.\n\nHere are some options:\n- **1000 mg PO three times daily** (3,000 mg/day), a common conservative regimen.\n- **1000 mg PO four times daily** (4,000 mg/day), the standard maximum.\n- **650 mg PO every 6 hours** (2,600 mg/day).\n\nIf 2000 mg was a typo, tell me the dose and frequency you want and I'll enter it. If you really intend 2000 mg three times daily, please confirm that, and I can add a note documenting the rationale. I'd still recommend checking with pharmacy first."
      }
     ],
